    {
      "id": "PY7EEKV86csP63BBvd5bT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -508.4650928891149,
      "y": 1941.0679348066485,
      "width": 878.8357543945312,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b50",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1148382660,
      "version": 109,
      "versionNonce": 2121569988,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786552329720,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How do we stream token back fast and keep stream smooth? ",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How do we stream token back fast and keep stream smooth? ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "mB2oalwSLnv8JwCXM1upJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -504.4396647686399,
      "y": 2001.8310782458868,
      "width": 1744.8049896239957,
      "height": 735,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b51",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2079677764,
      "version": 351,
      "versionNonce": 855806588,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786552757927,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-- SSE: The client opens an ordinary HTTP request with an EventSource. The server holds that response open and keeps\nwriting data: events to it as tokens are produced, and the browser fires an event for each one.\n-- SSE removes the browser-side polling delay, which settles the transport half of the responsiveness requirement. But it does\nnot explain how tokens reach the Chat Service instance holding that connection.\n-- Each token has to find its way from the worker that produced it to the Chat Service instance holding the user's SSE\nconnection right now. And the stream has to survive that instance being replaced mid-generation.\n-- Seperate with Redis Streams and Replay, replace Pub/Sub with Redis tream, which is append-only log.\n\nFull Flows:\n1) The Chat Service persists the user's message and mints a fresh runId, returning it to the client right away.\n2) The Chat Service hands the assembled prompt and that runId off to the Inference Service, which lines up a worker to\nstart generating (we'll put a queue here in the next deep dive).\n3) The client opens an SSE connection for that runId, and the load balancer routes it to any Chat Service instance.\n4) That Chat Service instance starts a blocking XREAD on the runId stream, from the beginning for a fresh connection or\nfrom the client's last-seen entry ID on a reconnect.\n5) The inference worker generates tokens and XADDs each one to the runId stream.\n6) The Chat Service instance reads new entries, forwards them down the SSE connection, and the browser appends them. If\nthe connection drops, the browser reconnects, lands on some Chat Service instance, and that instance replays from the last-\nseen ID before continuing live.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-- SSE: The client opens an ordinary HTTP request with an EventSource. The server holds that response open and keeps writing data: events to it as tokens are produced, and the browser fires an event for each one.\n-- SSE removes the browser-side polling delay, which settles the transport half of the responsiveness requirement. But it does not explain how tokens reach the Chat Service instance holding that connection.\n-- Each token has to find its way from the worker that produced it to the Chat Service instance holding the user's SSE connection right now. And the stream has to survive that instance being replaced mid-generation.\n-- Seperate with Redis Streams and Replay, replace Pub/Sub with Redis tream, which is append-only log.\n\nFull Flows:\n1) The Chat Service persists the user's message and mints a fresh runId, returning it to the client right away.\n2) The Chat Service hands the assembled prompt and that runId off to the Inference Service, which lines up a worker to start generating (we'll put a queue here in the next deep dive).\n3) The client opens an SSE connection for that runId, and the load balancer routes it to any Chat Service instance.\n4) That Chat Service instance starts a blocking XREAD on the runId stream, from the beginning for a fresh connection or from the client's last-seen entry ID on a reconnect.\n5) The inference worker generates tokens and XADDs each one to the runId stream.\n6) The Chat Service instance reads new entries, forwards them down the SSE connection, and the browser appends them. If the connection drops, the browser reconnects, lands on some Chat Service instance, and that instance replays from the last-seen ID before continuing live.\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
